"""The story of why each non-common instruction is in the instruction set, from the record of the design:
the author's questions in the original design chat (chat01.txt), the author's messages in the archive of the working session
(dates are 2026-10-21 and 2026-10-22), the revision changelogs of the instruction-set text (docs/pdp64-isa.md, docs/orig/), the errata
(docs/errata.md) and the measurements (docs/CHANGES-SINCE-R20.md section 14).  A common instruction (a move, an add, a branch: the
instructions every architecture has, and the PDP-11 core the machine started from) has no story and no entry here.
Every statement is in the sources it names; where the record gives no reason, the text says so instead of supplying one."""

STORY = {
'LEA': "The design chat opened with the questions \"why is SP in the register file but not PC?\", \"what is the problem with letting programmers read and modify PC directly?\" and \"what if R7 was a read-only view of the PC?\". The answer (ISA revision 13, section 9.3 of the book) was to keep the program counter out of the register file, because every register write would then be a potential branch, and to refuse the read-only view, which would fix for ever *which* PC is meant (ARM32 is stuck with `PC+8`). `LEA 0(PC), Rn` gives the capability without the commitment: the address of the instruction itself, in one instruction. The same instruction forms any address PC-relatively, which is what makes position-independent code free (section 9.16).",

'MOVU': "Data alignment is architectural on this machine: an ordinary quad access to an address that is not a multiple of 8 aborts, and that abort doubles as the tag check for tagged pointers (ISA sections 6.2 and 7.4; the rejection of \"a load form that ignores low pointer bits\" in section 11 gives the reason). Packed on-disk and network structures still need unaligned access, so `MESC` carries explicit unaligned forms rather than weakening the rule for every load. The record gives no further history for these four.",

'ADDM': "Through ISA revision 19 these were top-level opcodes `100000 (0x20)` to `100011 (0x23)`. Revision 20 reallocated all sixty-four opcodes by one rule (a top-level opcode is for an operation whose common case is register to register) and moved them to `MESC` at no cost, because an instruction with a memory destination is at least four bytes anyway; that freed four top-level slots. They cover the case where the second operand is a register, for example `BISM Rmask, flags(DP)` (ISA section 4.13). The measurement of 2026-10-22 found 265 sites in the compiled corpus (1.6 KB), so they earn their place in the escape group but not in the opcode space (this chat, the instruction-set analysis).",

'MULH': "ISA revision 17 pruned instructions that were carried for continuity and found one duplicate hiding a genuine hole: `MUL` and `MULU` were the same instruction, because the low 64 bits of a two's-complement product are identical signed or unsigned, so two ALU slots held one operation, while nothing in the architecture produced the *high* half. Without it a 64 by 64 to 128-bit multiply, every bignum multiply, 128-bit fixed point and divide-by-a-constant by multiplying by the reciprocal were impossible. `MULH` took the duplicate's slot (ISA revision 17, section 4.2.2). It is also why a divide is rare in compiled code: compilers turn division by a constant into `MULH` and a shift.",

'MULHU': "The unsigned partner of `MULH`, added in ISA revision 17 for the same reason (the high half of the product was missing). It went to `BESC` first and moved to `AESC` in revision 20, where arithmetic belongs. `MULHSU`, signed times unsigned for a bignum's top limb, was left reserved because it can be built from `MULH` with one correction.",

'ABS': "The author asked on 2026-10-21: \"do we have abs opcode?\" There was none for integers, only `FABS`. After the cost was worked out (about 150 to 250 lookup tables, no new operation by the folding rule, the compiler recognising `x < 0 ? -x : x` being the real cost) the author said \"add them, and if we have to teach the front end about abs/min/max nodes that is fine\". The measurement that followed found 18 uses of `abs` and 5 of its unsigned form in the compiled corpus, under 0.1% of code, so the case is clarity and the idiom more than size (errata 23; CHANGES section 14.4).",

'MINMAX': "Asked for together with `ABS` on 2026-10-21 (\"what would be the cost of adding abs/min/max for ints?\"; \"add them\"), as the integer counterparts of `FMIN` and `FMAX`. By the folding rule (integer against float and signed against unsigned are qualifiers) they add no operation, and the hardware reuses the subtract-and-compare that `CMP` already does plus a 64-bit select. The corpus has 171 uses of `MIN` and 52 of `MAX` across 95 files, under 0.1% of code in all (errata 23; CHANGES section 14.4). The alternative the assistant raised, a general conditional select, was left on hold for want of a speed model.",

'SET': "The first instruction written with a condition word: the author's questions on 2026-10-21 were \"why do them as single opcodes instead of as an opcode that takes a flag?\" and \"set eq instead of seteq\", which fixed the form as one instruction, `SET cc, Rd`, with the condition in a field numbered like the low nibble of the branch opcodes (errata 25). It turns a comparison used as a value into 0 or 1 without a branch. gcc `-Os` for amd64 emits 673 `set<cc>` on the corpus, and `SET` would remove about six bytes per site, around 0.4% of code; the compiler cannot yet reach it because pass 1 turns such a comparison into branches before pass 2 sees it (CHANGES section 14.4).",

'FSINGLE': "`binary64` has first-class opcodes because on a 64-bit machine it is the common case; `binary32` is in `FESC` with the same operations, held in the low 32 bits of a register and sign-extended like every other 32-bit value (ISA section 4.10, and the ABI). The old spellings (`FADDS` and so on) became aliases when the author fixed the naming rule on 2026-10-21: \"putting f in front for floating point ops is ok, `fadd` and I guess `fadd16`\" (errata 24).",

'FMADD': "ISA revision 13 added the whole fused multiply-add family to `FESC` because its absence \"was the single worst gap in the floating-point set\": one rounding instead of two, and the inner step of dot products, polynomial evaluation and every matrix kernel. `FESC` gives it four register operands (errata 22). It replaces the VAX `POLY`, which section 11 of the ISA calls the best answer before fused multiply-add and now superseded by it.",

'FMADD32': "The `binary32` forms of the fused multiply-add family, added with it in ISA revision 13.",

'FSIGN': "Part of the floating-point set of the instruction-set text (section 4.10, \"sign and selection\"): they work on the bits, do not canonicalise a NaN and raise no flag, so they cost almost nothing. When the author asked on 2026-10-21 what the PowerPC 601 had in floating point and \"any we may want to include?\", the comparison (made from memory and not checked against a manual) listed negate, absolute value and the like on the PowerPC side as well, and what the machine lacked was recorded in errata 17, 18 and 19. The record gives no separate reason for these three.",

'FMIN': "In the original floating-point set (ISA section 4.10), as the floating counterparts of the integer `MIN` and `MAX` added later on the author's request (errata 23). A NaN operand loses to a number and two NaNs give the canonical quiet NaN (errata 26, decided in `sim64`).",

'FCLASS': "\"Every libm function begins with this dispatch\" (ISA section 4.10): one instruction returns which of ten kinds a value is (signalling or quiet NaN, infinity, normal, subnormal, zero, each signed), where software needs several compares and masks. It is in the original floating-point set; the assistant listed it among the items justified by a workload and not by compiled C, together with the tag branches, and recommended waiting for the frequency table before cutting any (this chat, 2026-10-21). Nothing in the corpus emits it, because the compiler is not taught to.",

'FRINT': "Rounding to an integral value in each direction (nearest even, toward zero, down, up) is in the original set. **`FRINTA`, ties away from zero, was added on 2026-10-21** after the PowerPC 601 comparison, for C's `round()`, which is common in libm; it is a qualifier of the round operation and so costs no new operation (errata 19). The author's reaction: \"add those 3 things, i like having the rounding variants\" (the other two were the exception flags and the unsigned conversions).",

'FCVT': "The conversions were in the original set. **`FCVTDQU` and `FCVTQUD`, unsigned 64-bit to and from `binary64`, were added on 2026-10-21** because C needs both and without them each takes a branch and several instructions; the author approved them with the other two additions (\"add those 3 things\"; errata 18).",

'FSFLAGS': "The one thing missing for IEEE 754 conformance, found on 2026-10-21 while answering the author's question about the PowerPC 601's floating-point operations: a search of the instruction-set text for flags, `fenv`, \"inexact\" and \"sticky\" found nothing, yet IEEE requires five status flags and C99 `<fenv.h>` needs software access to them. The fix is a sticky five-bit `FPSR`, laid out as RISC-V's `fflags`, written by every operation that can raise a flag and never trapping, and one swap instruction to read and write it. It is a record of what happened, not a mode, so the rule against mode bits is untouched. The author approved it with \"add those 3 things\" (; errata 17).",

'FHALF': "The author raised half precision on 2026-10-21 (the assistant's answer opens \"Is FP16 a GPU thing?\" and goes on \"You're right about the gradients\"). The answer, from memory and not checked against a manual, was that it began as one (NVIDIA's `half`, 2002), spread to machine learning and image formats, and is now in CPUs (x86 `F16C` and AVX-512 FP16, ARMv8.2, RISC-V `Zfh` and `Zfhmin`). The assistant laid out two levels, conversions only (about 500 to 1,000 lookup tables, no new operation by the folding rule) and full arithmetic (about 2,000 to 3,500 tables, estimates of its own), and noted that the existing double datapath can compute half correctly rounded. On 2026-10-22 the author decided: \"add fp16 conversions and arithmetic\". The leftovers (half `FMIN`, `FRINT*`, bfloat16, 64-bit integer conversions) are open items. The related question of 16-bit *integer* arithmetic was treated as a design item and decided against (errata 28).",

'FCVTH': "The conversion half of the `binary16` decision (see `FHALF`): on 2026-10-22 the author asked for \"fp16 conversions and arithmetic\". The conversions are the part machine-learning code needs most, because a half is normally loaded and stored with `MOVW`, converted, computed in single or double, and converted back.",

'COUNT': "ISA revision 13 restored a bit-manipulation group, `BESC`, because revision 10's reorganisation had silently dropped `BSWAP`, `EXTB`, `INSB` and `MOD` (\"a plain bug\"): bit manipulation is not an extension on a machine that does filesystem work, bignum arithmetic and the like. They restore the VAX `FFS` and `FFC` idiom (find first set, find first clear; ISA section 14.1). LoongArch was the model for putting them in the base: it has `CLO`, `CLZ`, `CTO`, `CTZ` there, and that was \"the decisive argument for giving it a top-level opcode\" (ISA section 15.1). The compiler emits none of them yet, since C has no operator for them; the corpus shows zero uses (CHANGES section 14.4).",

'POPCNT': "One of the counts restored with `BESC` in ISA revision 13; it is also the building block of `XNORPOP`. A compiler needs `__builtin_popcount` to emit it, so the corpus shows no use (see `COUNT`).",

'BSWAP': "The PDP-11 `SWAB` swapped the bytes of a word; revision 13 restored it generalised to 64 bits, with word and long variants, after revision 10 had dropped it by accident. `SWAB` is therefore subsumed, not lost (ISA section 14.3). ",

'BITREV': "Part of the `BESC` group of revision 13, borrowed with the other LoongArch bit operations (`REVB`, `REVH`, `BITREV`, ISA section 15.1). The record gives no workload from the corpus.",

'ROR': "The question that started revision 16 was why the ALU group had `ROR` but not `ROL`. The answer was that one rotate direction suffices and `ROR` is the one to keep: `ROL x, #n` is exactly `ROR x, #(64-n)`, and a funnel shifter yields `ROR` directly (ISA section 4.5.1). Revision 16 also stated for the first time that no rotate is through carry. In revision 20 `ROR` was demoted from the ALU group to `BESC`, \"accepted, and revision 16's justification for its ALU slot was simply wrong\": crypto rotates by compile-time constants, so a four-byte immediate form is needed either way. On 2026-10-22 the author took the recommendation that rotates write no flags (, errata 39).",

'RORI': "Added with the register-count rotate in revision 16 and given its own mnemonic in errata 10, because an immediate count and a register count are different encodings. `ROL Rs, #n, Rd` is accepted by the assembler as `RORI Rs, #(64-n), Rd` (ISA revision 17, section 4.15): the complement costs nothing at assembly time, so there is no reason to make programmers do it, and a register-count `ROL` is rejected so that one mnemonic stays one instruction.",

'RORI32': "Added on 2026-10-22 with the second page (errata 31): a 32-bit rotate by a constant, the shape of crypto and hash inner loops (revision 20 had noted that crypto rotates by compile-time constants), estimated together with `CBCC` at 22 KB (1.3%) of the compiled image. Its first specification zero-extended the result; the author decided \"make rori32 sign-extend like the rest\", so that no instruction is needed after it (errata 34). It removed 44 `MOVL` instructions in the corpus, the demand analysis having already removed nearly all of them.",

'DSHR': "Added in ISA revision 16: a double-width funnel shift (x86 `SHRD`, ARM `EXTR`), the right primitive for multi-word shifting. Two instructions shift a 128-bit value by any distance, where rotate-through-carry took one bit per instruction, and that is why no rotate is through carry. It also subsumes `ROR` when both sources are the same register. It leaves every flag alone.",

'EXTB': "Revision 10's reorganisation dropped bit-field extract and insert by accident; revision 13 restored them (the VAX `EXTV`, `EXTZV` and `INSV` idiom, ISA section 14.1). The compiler does not use them: the shift-pair extraction pattern occurs 151 times in 26 files of the corpus, about 600 bytes at best (0.03%), and there are four true bit-field extracts in all. They are for hand-written protocol and bit-stream code (CHANGES section 14.4).",

'INSB': "Restored with `EXTB` in revision 13 (the VAX `INSV` idiom). The record names no use in the compiled corpus.",

'XNORPOP': "Added in ISA revision 13 in answer to the author's design-chat question about the new language models that use single-bit encodings (\"are there instruction sequences that would benefit from coalescing?\"). Revision 14 corrected the reason: the instruction does **not** serve sub-1-bit language models, because the activations in the LittleBit work are FP16 and the inner loop is a conditional add, not a popcount. It stays for what it does serve: binary-activation networks, Hamming distance, bitset intersection counts and counting a collector's mark bitmap. The sub-1-bit kernel is bandwidth-bound and belongs to a bus-mastering accelerator, not an opcode (ISA section 4.6.2; chapter 8).",

'CLMUL': "ISA revision 15 added carry-less multiply as the primitive beneath every CRC polynomial (a CRC is a carry-less multiply plus a Barrett reduction), GF(2^k) erasure codes and AES-GCM, chosen over a `CRC` instruction because x86 and ARM each committed a polynomial to the architecture. On 2026-10-21 the author asked \"how about the 2 crc variants also?\", the assistant added eight `CRC32`/`CRC32C` words, and the author withdrew them at: \"ok, we have carryless mul no need for either crc\" (errata 20).",

'UTF8LEN': "The design chat asked \"do we have any special instructions for utf8?\" and later whether the \"string\" functions being omitted included `utf8enc` and `utf8dec`. ISA revision 13 added three, because most of what UTF-8 needs is general bit manipulation but \"the validity rules are where the bugs are\". `UTF8LEN` is the exception that is nearly free: `CLO` on the leading byte, mapped to 1, 2, 3 or 4 (ISA section 4.6.1).",

'UTF8DEC': "Added in ISA revision 13 (the design chat: \"do we have any special instructions for utf8?\"). Not for speed but for correctness: overlong forms, surrogates, values above U+10FFFF and the bytes that can never begin a sequence are each a historical security hole, each is three comparators in hardware, and software implementations get them wrong or omit them for speed (ISA section 4.6.1, about 300 lookup tables).",

'UTF8ENC': "The encoding half of the UTF-8 pair added in ISA revision 13; it refuses surrogates and values above U+10FFFF so that an encoder cannot produce what a decoder must reject.",

'MCPY': "The design chat's questions: \"maybe memory to memory mov is useful and should be allowed, especially with a loop\" and \"principles exceptions are allowed where there is a clear benefit\" (which produced the exception rule of ISA section 1.1), then \"is there any indirect addressing, so you could load r1 and r2 with addresses then `rep movsb r1,r2,10` to mov 10 bytes. and maybe an instruction to zero out a range of memory. is the cbnz idiom better? how about mmov mcpy mset like ibm system z?\". ISA revision 13 answered with `MBLK`: `MCPY`, `MMOV`, `MSET`, `MCMP`, `MSCN`, the one granted exception to the one-memory-operand rule, made safe by keeping the progress in architectural registers (the VAX `MOVC5` kept it in hidden microstate, which is why it was rejected; ARM reached the same design in 2022 with `FEAT_MOPS`). `MCPY` replaces the PDP-11 `MOV (R1)+,(R2)+` loop with one restartable instruction.",

'MSET': "Asked for in the design chat (\"maybe an instruction to zero out a range of memory\") and delivered in ISA revision 13 with `MCPY`; with a zero value it is the range-zeroing instruction, so `bzero` and the clearing of `calloc` need no separate opcode.",

'MCMP': "The design chat asked about the \"string\" functions being omitted: \"doesn't mcmp/mmov count?\". It did: ISA revision 15 corrected the book-keeping, because the rejection of \"string\" instructions was wrong from revision 8 and visibly wrong by revision 13. Seven of the VAX's ten character-string instructions are present (`MOVC3`, `CMPC3`, `LOCC` and `SKPC` among them), and the rejection was always about `EDITPC` and packed decimal. `MCMP` is the `CMPC3` equivalent.",

'MSCN': "`MSCN` is `LOCC` and `memchr`; **`MSCNN` was added in ISA revision 15 to fill the `SKPC` gap** (the first byte that is *not* equal). Both cost no opcode and almost no hardware. With an explicit count `MSCN` gives `strnlen`, and with `MCPY` a bounded `strcpy` in two instructions. NUL-terminated string instructions (the System/z `MVST`, `SRST`, `CLST`) are explicitly rejected, because they encode an unbounded read, the largest single source of memory-safety failures in C (ISA sections 11 and 14.2).",

'MVAL': "The design chat asked \"what is mblk? what is a swar? validating an entire utf8 string sounds nice... do we do it often enough to justify the opcode? if utf8 is already 3 or 4 parcels, it's ok.\" ISA revision 15 committed `MVAL`: the UTF-8 validity comparators already built for `UTF8DEC`, wired to the `MBLK` streaming sequencer already built for `MCPY`, so it costs no opcode and almost no hardware. The alternative, a SWAR loop (SIMD within a register), runs at about two to three cycles a byte and is respectable, which is why the justification is cost and not throughput.",

'SOB': "Restored from the PDP-11 in ISA revision 13 at the branch group's one free opcode. The PDP-11's could only branch backward (a 6-bit unsigned displacement); this one branches either way. It answers the design chat's question about the \"cbnz idiom\": it does not save bytes (`DEC` plus `BNE` is also four) but it saves an instruction issue, a cycle per iteration on a machine that does not pipeline. The measurement of 2026-10-22: \"same size, one instruction fewer\" (static `dec; bne` pairs exist).",

'CBCC': "The author asked on 2026-10-22: \"why do we need 8 byte instructions? only amd64 has more than 6 bytes. can we squeeze into 3 parcels?\" The fourth parcel was removed and the freed length code became the second page (errata 31); at the same time a fused compare-and-branch, `CBCC`, was added in the reserved opcode `101000 (0x28)`, estimated with `RORI32` at 22 KB (1.3%) of the image. Then, measured, step by step: the author asked for the wider form (\"do the wider cbcc\") and the **wide-immediate `CBCC`** removed 11,735 instructions (3.4%) and 1,828 bytes; \"do the register-register compare-and-branch\" added **`CBRR`**, which removed 4,631 instructions (1.4%) and 142 bytes (errata 34 and 35). The reason it is not a replacement for flags: LoongArch and RISC-V chose fused compare-and-branch instead of flags, and the ISA declines that (section 15.2) because it needs a comparator in the branch unit and a third read port; here the fused form is added beside the flags. Errata 39 then made `CBCC` write no flags.",

'BTAG': "The design chat asked: \"what instructions are there that make using the lowtag stuff easy for lisp type things?\" ISA revision 13 answered with the tag branches `BTAG` and `BNTAG` and the table dispatch `JTAG`, \"to make tagged pointers cheap to use rather than merely possible\": three bits of tag and one of sense fit the `low` nibble exactly, so parcel 1 is a full displacement. Because quad-aligned objects have zero in the low three bits, the tag is free, and an unaligned access is a fault. The compiler for C never emits them (the corpus shows none); they are for Lisp and other tagged runtimes.",

'JTAG': "The table-dispatch partner of `BTAG`, added in ISA revision 13 for the same question about tagged Lisp pointers. It is legal only in the scaled-index mode (errata 6), the one mode with a free register slot for the tagged value. It replaces the PDP-11 dispatch-table idiom `@(Rn)+` better, because it needs no pointer register and cannot index out of range (ISA section 14.2).",

'JMPI': "ISA revision 18 reintroduced exactly one deferred reference, for control transfer only, in `XFER`: the PLT stub of every dynamically linked program (`JMPI got_slot(PC)`) and virtual-method dispatch (`CALLI vtbl_off(Robj)`), each two instructions and a scratch register without it. It is admitted because it breaks none of the reasons deferred modes were removed (no register is side-effected, the reference is in the branch path and not the operand path, a fault is clean by ordering; ISA section 4.9.1). The measurement of 2026-10-22: jump tables through `JMPI` are not smaller, because the table entries are 8 bytes against 8 for a case in a compare chain.",

'PUSHM': "The VAX procedure entry mask was the useful half of `CALLS`; ISA revision 13 kept that half as `PUSHM` and `POPM` without the frame (which the book rejects: hardware call frames ran slower than the sequence on later VAXes). They began in `MBLK` and moved to `SOP` because a 16-bit mask does not fit beside `MBLK`'s sub-opcode; that used `SOP`'s last two free slots (CHANGES section 3.2). The second page has 8-bit forms for `R8` to `R15`, because all 2,392 push sites and 2,392 pop sites in the measured corpus used a mask within `R8` to `R15`.",

'CAS': "ISA revision 13 expanded the atomic escape with barrier-fused variants, taken from LoongArch's pragmatism: a lock acquire is one instruction rather than an atomic followed by a `FENCE`, and that pair is in every critical section of the system. The design chat asked what is needed for symmetric multiprocessing and what can be learned from modern multi-core processors; revision 15 added section 17 (a sequentially consistent model, nearly free on an in-order machine) and the `SYS` additions. The author's question on 2026-10-21, \"how many folded opcodes in system and smp? i thought atomics and smp would be in the same umbrella\", made the seventeen-operation SMP umbrella. On 2026-10-22 the author chose atomics defined on a reservation monitor and not a bus lock (decision P11, errata 45). The group is specified and not yet implemented in the reference executor.",

'SWAP': "One of the read-modify-write atomics of the `ATOM` group (see `CAS`). The record gives no separate history for it.",

'XADD': "One of the fetch-and-operate atomics of `ATOM` (see `CAS`), in the place of the PDP-11's read-modify-write instructions, which were indivisible on the Unibus through the `DATIP`/`DATO` pair (ISA section 9.13).",

'XAND': "The fetch-and-and, or and xor atomics of `ATOM` (see `CAS`). The record gives no separate history for them.",

'XMAX': "The fetch-and-minimum and maximum atomics, borrowed from LoongArch's richer atomic set (ISA section 15.1): each is a couple of hundred lookup tables and saves a compare-and-swap retry loop.",

'LLSC': "Load-linked and store-conditional, with the reservation made a monitor in errata 45 (the author's pick, decision P11, 2026-10-22), because a monitor maps onto the exclusive access of AXI and of FPGA and modern memory systems, where the Unibus's locked read-modify-write (`DATIP`/`DATO`) does not scale as cores multiply. A short sequence must eventually succeed if no other initiator keeps writing the granule, the RISC-V rule.",

'NOP': "`NOP` is common and has no story; `LPAD` is the landing pad. Landing pads, `LPAD`, mark the legal targets of indirect jumps and calls so that jump-oriented attacks can be stopped at the transfer, as Intel's indirect-branch tracking and ARM's branch target identification do and OpenBSD enforces. ABI version 1 leaves them off (an open item of the instruction set, section 12.3 of the text), and the assembler provides `.lpad`; the kernel pain-points and mitigations reports (this chat, 2026-10-22) record why deciding later gets more expensive (appendix A, section A.1).",

'RTI': "`RTT` stays despite looking redundant against `RTI` (ISA revision 17): it sets the trace bit *after* the return rather than before, which is what lets a debugger single-step out of its own trap handler.",

'BPT': "`BPT` is just a trap vector, but a debugger patches it over an existing instruction, so it must be the shortest instruction the machine has; that is why it stayed when `IOT` and `EMT` were dropped in ISA revision 17.",

'FENCE': "ISA revision 15 declared the memory model sequentially consistent and so needs `FENCE` only for what is outside the model: devices and the framebuffer (one `FENCE` between writing a device's registers and setting its go bit, section 3.4 of the book) and instruction streams. The record says the `_DB` barrier forms of the atomics were added so that a `FENCE` is not needed in a lock acquire.",

'WFE': "`WAIT` was a PDP-11 instruction (sleep until an interrupt) and was dropped in ISA revision 17 as subsumed by `WFE`, which sleeps until an interrupt or another core's `SEV`; on a uniprocessor it behaves exactly as `WAIT` did. `WFE` and `SEV` came with the SMP additions of revision 15 (the design chat's question about symmetric multiprocessing). The wake rule (whether a pending but masked interrupt wakes it) was an accidental decision that the author asked to see written up like the others; it is open item P1.",

'SEV': "The signalling half of `WFE`, added in ISA revision 15 so that a core waiting for a lock can sleep instead of spinning and be woken by the core that releases it.",

'TLBA': "ISA revision 15 respecified `TLBI` and `TLBIA` as broadcasting and added `TLBSYNC` to wait for the other cores, and `ICINV` and `ICINVA`: \"the absence of an I-cache invalidate through revision 14 was a hole\", because a Lisp runtime that compiles a function cannot work without it.",

'PUSHX': "The author decided on 2026-10-22: \"add pushx popx\", then \"i like the pushx popx savings; they might not show up high in the instruction count, but they'd save bytes and cycles in a very tight, very often used kernel loop, no?\" A handler that can be interrupted must save `EPC` and `EPSW` before it lowers the priority; with only `MFSR` that costs two moves and two stores (sixteen bytes) on every such kernel entry, and the same to restore. It came out of the System v1 design (errata 36 and 37). Page 2 has the two-byte forms.",

'ISYNC': "Adopted on 2026-10-22 (errata 39) from the out-of-order readiness review, which the author asked for (\"i'm ok with the fpga/asic being in order, but want to be ready for the out of order large scale design\"): it makes earlier writes to `PTBR`, `ASID`, `MMUCTL` and `VBR` take effect for what follows, so an in-order machine may make them immediate and a speculative one has a place to serialise.",

'HINT': "Adopted with `ISYNC` on 2026-10-22 (errata 39): a no-op with a byte of code, defined for `0` (plain), `1` (pause, for spin-wait loops) and `2` (speculation barrier), every other code a reserved hint that executes as a no-op so that a later machine can give it a meaning without breaking an old binary.",

'MFSR': "The author asked on 2026-10-22: \"actually didn't you notice some missing instructions that a paging os needs? you should add them in now to, to the docs, the grammar, and the backend\". The instruction-set text named `PTBR`, `EPC`, `EVA` and `ECAUSE` and had no instruction to write the first or read the others, so a kernel could neither install a page table nor learn why it was entered. One general pair reaches all of them by name (errata 32). The user and supervisor stack pointers are in the set because any kernel must read and write the user stack pointer to build a signal frame, copy a process or switch context.",

'SPL': "`SPL` stays despite `MTPS` writing the whole status word (ISA revision 17): it sets the priority alone, leaving the condition codes untouched, so a kernel raising priority around a critical section does not clobber flags. It is the PDP-11 instruction of the same name.",

'TRAP': "`TRAPL` was added (errata 5) so that the vector range of 256 and the two-byte common case could both be kept: `TRAP #n` for `n` below 16 is two bytes and the long form reaches 256. `IOT` and `EMT`, which were `TRAP` at two particular vectors on the PDP-11, were dropped in ISA revision 17.",

'TLBI': "Respecified as broadcasting in ISA revision 15 (SMP section): a translation changed by one core must be invalidated on all of them, and `TLBSYNC` waits for it.",

'MTASID': "The `ASID` tags translation-buffer entries so a context switch is one store and no flush, which preserves the constant-time context switch the earlier map-set design was for (ISA section 8.1). The record gives no further history.",

'MTDP': "`DP` entered at revision 1 as the descendant of the 6502's zero page, kept for code density (a 12-bit displacement in four bytes); it turned out to be the thread pointer in user mode and, in kernel mode, the per-CPU pointer that an SMP kernel wants (ISA sections 2.2 and 19.3). `MTDP` and `MFDP` read and write the current mode's copy.",

'MTDMW': "Taken from LoongArch's direct-mapping windows (ISA section 15.1), which stop the kernel taking translation-buffer misses on its own text. The author decided on 2026-10-22 that the windows are deferred, `MMUCTL` stays, and the instruction is storage only for now (errata 44).",

'MFCPUID': "Added in ISA revision 15 with the SMP section, because per-CPU data needs each core to know which one it is.",

'ICINV': "Added with `ICINVA` in ISA revision 15: instruction-cache invalidation, by address and whole. The reason is the one given for `TLBA`: a runtime that compiles a function cannot work without it.",

'PROBE': "Restored from the VAX `PROBER` and `PROBEW` in the idioms the revision 13 inventory brought back (ISA section 14.1): the kernel validates a user address before using it, and it replaces the `nofault` global of the V7 kernel for the address-validation case.",
}
